LL='https://www.livelaw.in/amp/sc-refuses-interim-relief-dlf-orders-pay-rs-630-crore-cci-penalty-interest-bearing-fixed-deposit-court'
TRIB15='https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/archive/features/cag-report-vindicates-my-stand-says-khemka-58884'
TRIB24='https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/haryana/dlf-robert-vadra-deal-khemka-questions-slow-pace-of-probe-verma-hits-back-608239'
WLG='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DLF_land_grab_case'
WKP='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kushal_Pal_Singh'
add('kushal-pal-singh','mixed',
 'Singh led DLF, a Gurugram land developer. The record has four separate strands, none of which is a conviction of Singh: (1) a 2014 SEBI order, in force for six months or less before being quashed by a split Tribunal in 2015, about DLF\'s 2007 IPO disclosures (Singh was a named noticee); (2) a CCI abuse-of-dominance penalty on DLF (company, not Singh); (3) the Vadra-DLF Manesar land sale of 2008 and the Haryana licensing allegations, where the reviewed sources concern DLF, Robert Vadra and the then Haryana government, and report no finding against Singh personally; and (4) reported long-standing Congress ties. Together they show a land developer operating inside a state licensing system, but not a documented personal exchange. No rubric is assigned.',
 'State land licensing and development approvals; Vadra-DLF land sale alleged but not adjudicated against Singh',
 'DLF IPO 2007 and SEBI proceedings 2011–2020; CCI 2011–2014; Vadra-DLF transaction 2008 and Haryana proceedings 2012–2026',
 [r('SEBI\'s whole-time member restrained DLF, K. P. Singh and five other executives from the securities market for three years, in an order dated 10 October 2014. The order found, among other things, that DLF had suppressed information about three entities (Sudipti, Felicite, Shalika) in its 2007 IPO documents. It arose from complaints by Kimsuk Krishna Sinha, which the Delhi High Court had directed SEBI to investigate. It records the company and the individuals disputing the charges. No monetary penalty was imposed in this order.','SEBI order WTM/RKA/IVD-7/117-124/2014, DLF Ltd and others',SEBI14,'2014-10-10','Regulatory finding (later set aside); person named','PDF full text extracted and read directly'),
  r('Tribunal (SAT) in Appeal 331 of 2014 and connected appeals, decision 13 March 2015, bench J. P. Devadhar (Presiding Officer), Jog Singh and A. S. Lamba. The majority held that DLF did not commit fraud on investors and that all relevant material aspects were in the offer documents, and set the order aside. The Presiding Officer dissented, accepting that DLF had used sham share transfers to conceal association with three companies, but found the alleged knowledge of an FIR unsupported and would have reduced the restraint to six months from 10 October 2014.','SAT judgment, DLF Ltd v SEBI and connected appeals',SAT15,'2015-03-13','Appellate judgment, split decision','PDF text extracted; majority and minority passages read, not the whole 390,000 characters'),
  r('Company filing: SEBI appealed under section 15Z; the Supreme Court admitted the appeals on 24 April 2015, granted no stay, and notice issued on interim applications. In October 2015 SEBI sought to restrain a sale of promoter-group preference shares; the Court on 4 November 2015 made no restraining order. DLF states it deposited Rs 630 crore with the Supreme Court in the CCI matter.','DLF Limited, standalone results note 4, quarter ended June 2020',DLFQ1,'2020','Company disclosure of litigation status; company characterisation','PDF text extracted and read; company-authored'),
  r('Supreme Court order in the Sinha appeals (Civil Appeals 4089-4096 of 2015): disposed of as withdrawn on 25 August 2020, Hrishikesh Roy J. in chamber; the order says it does not affect any other proceedings. Why the appeals were withdrawn is not stated in the summary read.','Supreme Court order via Casemine',SC20,'2020-08-25','Court order (procedural), secondary host','Page summary only; full order text not read'),
  r('The CCI penalised DLF Rs 630 crore (August 2011) for abusing dominance over apartment buyers after a Belaire owners\' complaint; COMPAT upheld the penalty on 19 May 2014. The Supreme Court on 27 August 2014 admitted DLF\'s appeal and required the amount to be deposited in an interest-bearing deposit. This is a company penalty on contract terms with buyers, not a political-influence finding.','Business Today; LiveLaw',BT,'2014-05-19','Company-level competition finding','Both pages read via fetch summary; final outcome in the Supreme Court not found'),
  r('CAG report for 2013-14 tabled in the Haryana Assembly on 25 March 2015 criticised the Town and Country Planning Department for not capturing for the exchequer developers\' profit above 15 percent when licences were approved and transferred, letting developers earn large profits merely by selling land. It did not name Vadra but named his company Skylight Hospitality, which in 2008 sold a 3.5-acre Manesar plot to DLF for Rs 58 crore. The reporting is Tribune\'s account of the CAG report; the report itself was not read.','Tribune News Service, 26 March 2015',TRIB15,'2015-03-26','Audit finding as reported (secondary)','Article text reviewed; CAG report not fetched'),
  r('Chronology as stated by the page: Skylight bought about 3.5 acres for Rs 7.5 crore in 2008, a housing project was permitted days later by the Congress-led Haryana government, and DLF agreed to buy it for Rs 58 crore; the mutation was cancelled by Ashok Khemka in October 2012 and he was transferred; FIR 288 of 1 September 2018 at Kherki Daula named Hooda, Vadra, DLF and Onkareshwar Properties; the ED filed a prosecution complaint in July 2025 alleging Vadra received Rs 58 crore. Allegations only, with the case in trial. The page does not show any charge against Singh personally.','Wikipedia: DLF land grab case',WLG,'2026',"Tertiary chronology of allegations and proceedings",WIKI)],
 [r('SAT\'s majority found no fraud on investors and set aside the SEBI order; SEBI\'s appeals were withdrawn in 2020 (the Sinha appeals) with no Supreme Court merits decision found. DLF and the individuals contested every charge before SEBI.','SAT judgment; Supreme Court order',SAT15,'2015-03-13','Contrary adjudication and responses','Judgment text read'),
  r('The Tribune records that the Hooda government had earlier given Vadra a clean chit, that the CAG did not name Vadra, and that a Haryana Government statement to the High Court in 2023 reportedly found no rule violated. The 2024 article reports no statement from DLF or Vadra, that the Dhingra commission report was quashed by the High Court for procedural flaws, and that the case is still unresolved.','Tribune News Service, April 2024',TRIB24,'2024-04-06','Absence of outcome and conflicting accounts','Article read via fetch summary'),
  r('The Wikipedia biography reports Singh\'s close ties to the Congress party and Nehru-Gandhi family as a recurring source of controversy, and that Kejriwal alleged corruption in 2011. These are characterisations and allegations, not findings, and the biography is tertiary.','Wikipedia: Kushal Pal Singh',WKP,'2026','Contested characterisation',WIKI)],
 'Not a legal or sanctions opinion. Primary documents: SEBI 2014 order, SAT 2015 judgment, DLF\'s own filing. Not obtained: the CAG report itself, the Dhingra commission report (not public), the Haryana FIR, the ED complaint, DLF\'s and Vadra\'s statements on the land deal, and the final outcome of the CCI appeal. SEBI\'s later penalty orders were not read. Search capacity was exhausted midway. The Vadra-DLF matter concerns a political family and a developer, not charges against Singh; the benefit to Vadra (Rs 7.5 crore to Rs 58 crore) is a company-level transaction. Not exoneration, not an allegation of personal wrongdoing.')

# ---------------- Uday Kotak
